{
  "teamId": 136,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Seattle Mariners entered the season with genuine playoff hopes, riding a roster built around Nelson Cruz's power, Robinson Canó's steadiness, and the electric young arm of Edwin Díaz in the bullpen. But from the very first week, the warning signs were there — George Springer's three-run blast off Chase De Jong on April 5th set a tone of vulnerability that would haunt the club all year. A brutal April collapse left them 11-15 before they had even found their footing.\n\nMay and June brought a genuine resurgence. Sparks from unlikely heroes like Taylor Motter, Jarrod Dyson, and Guillermo Heredia fueled winning stretches, and Mike Zunino's walk-off-caliber two-run homer on June 7th was the emotional peak of the season — a moment that briefly made Mariners fans believe this group had turned a corner. The team went 15-12 in June and 14-12 in July, playing well enough to keep the playoff dream alive deep into summer.\n\nJuly and August told a story of inches. Jean Segura's clutch single against Boston, Leonys Martin's timely home run, and Yonder Alonso's big blast kept the Mariners competitive, but Edwin Díaz — so often brilliant — was caught up in several of the season's most damaging moments, surrendering leads at the worst possible times. The bullpen as a whole was a revolving door of heartbreak, and a squad that scored 750 runs while allowing 772 was never quite good enough to sustain a run.\n\nSeptember exposed the team's ceiling. The Mariners went 12-15 down the stretch, watching their playoff hopes dissolve game by game. A late three-run homer from Danny Valencia offered one last flicker of defiance, but a Luis Valbuena double off Díaz on September 29th encapsulated the season's cruel pattern perfectly — the closer Seattle leaned on most was also the source of their most damaging defeats. At 78-84, the Mariners extended their playoff drought to 16 years, a franchise stuck in an agonizing middle ground between contention and rebuilding.",
